Gastro-oesophageal Reflux Disease (GORD)
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Retrosternal heartburn, Acid regurgitation, Exacerbation when supine, Relief with antacids

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-025
Reason for Visit: Severe, burning chest pain radiating to the throat that woke the patient from sleep, causing extreme panic regarding a potential heart attack.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe, acute-onset retrosternal chest pain waking a patient from sleep. Urgent out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was strictly mandated to rapidly evaluate for acute coronary syndrome, perform an ECG, and definitively distinguish between ischemic cardiac pain and severe esophageal spasm/reflux prior to the availability of routine general practice services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up choking with a terrible burning pressure in my chest, and I am terrified I'm having a heart attack."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports severe retrosternal burning chest pain that began 2 hours after falling asleep, immediately following a very large, late, high-fat meal and alcohol consumption. Reports regurgitation of sour and highly acidic gastric contents into the mouth upon waking. Describes water brash (sudden appearance of salty fluid). Notes a chronic non-productive cough, particularly at night, and persistent throat clearing in the morning. Symptoms are severely exacerbated when lying supine or bending over. Reports partial relief of symptoms following administration of liquid antacids.
Functional Status: Pacing the room anxiously, refusing to lie down due to fear of worsening the chest pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding cardiovascular risk; living alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 92 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious and uncomfortable, frequently burping and clearing throat. No diaphoresis, pallor, or shortness of breath.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals regular heart sounds with no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. 12-lead ECG performed on-site shows normal sinus rhythm with no ST-segment or T-wave abnormalities indicative of ischemia. Chest is clear to auscultation bilaterally. Abdomen is soft with mild epigastric tenderness on deep palpation, but no guarding or rebound. The presentation clearly matches the tetrad: retrosternal heartburn, acid regurgitation, exacerbation when supine, and relief with antacids.
Environmental Observations: Empty pizza boxes and wine glasses on the living room table.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. Consumed half a bottle of over-the-counter liquid antacid tonight.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Gastro-oesophageal Reflux Disease (GORD) - Status: Exacerbated (Acute severe nocturnal episode)
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome (Myocardial Infarction) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The completely normal 12-lead ECG during active pain, absence of diaphoresis or radiation to the arm/jaw, and the specific triggering of symptoms by a large meal and recumbency.

Peptic Ulcer Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain being primarily retrosternal and characterized by acidic regurgitation into the oropharynx, rather than isolated, gnawing epigastric pain.

Eosinophilic Oesophagitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of solid food dysphagia or a history of recurrent food bolus impaction requiring endoscopic removal.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe retrosternal chest pain waking them from sleep, a classic high-risk scenario necessitating immediate medical evaluation to exclude myocardial infarction. An urgent after-hours home visit and on-site ECG safely and definitively ruled out acute cardiac ischemia. Clinical assessment confirmed the pain was driven by severe nocturnal acid reflux secondary to a late, heavy meal and loss of gravitational clearance while supine. Diagnosing this non-cardiac etiology at the bedside prevented an unnecessary, highly stressful emergency ambulance dispatch and hospital admission, safely resolving the patient's acute anxiety with targeted high-dose acid suppression.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral viscous lidocaine/antacid suspension (e.g., "Gastric Cocktail") with immediate resolution of the burning pain. Prescribed Esomeprazole 40mg daily for 4 weeks.
Positive Behavioral Change: Elevate the head of your bed using 15cm solid blocks under the top bed legs. By mechanically raising your upper body, you use gravity to physically trap the stomach acid below the esophageal sphincter while you sleep, preventing the acid from washing up into your chest and causing these severe nighttime attacks.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bed risers or a specialized wedge pillow.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 4 weeks to assess response to the proton pump inhibitor.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the chest pain returns and feels crushing or heavy, radiates to your left arm or jaw, or is accompanied by a cold sweat and shortness of breath, call 000 immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: While your ECG is normal today, any new or different type of severe chest pain must always be evaluated as a potential cardiac event.
Clinical Course: The daily medication will stop your stomach from producing acid, allowing the chemical burns in your esophagus to heal over the next few weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your heart tracing is completely normal. The pain is originating from acid burning your food pipe. However, if you develop difficulty swallowing solid food, or if you ever vomit material that looks like coffee grounds, you must see a doctor urgently.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The valve separating your stomach and your chest relaxed after your large, late meal. When you lay flat, the highly acidic stomach contents flowed backward, causing severe chemical burns to the sensitive lining of your chest tube. The prescribed medication completely blocks the acid pumps in your stomach, giving the tissue time to heal.
Actionable Steps: Do not eat any food or drink alcohol within three hours of going to bed. Eating late fills the stomach and increases the pressure against the valve; going to sleep with an empty stomach ensures there is no volume of food or acid physically available to reflux into your chest.

